## 2026-10-25 — A referral must change a decision, and each intervention needs one place in a reply

**Development candidate, not deployed.** Owner-directed response lessons from a supervisor edit: after repeatedly ineffective or coercive medical encounters, a new routine referral needs a concrete attainable purpose rather than just another general checkup; a new serious acute impairment remains separately urgent, with a realistic explanation of what could be treated or ruled out. First ask what a person observed from a helpful practice, rather than adding an unsupported dissociation warning. Preserve the boundary between dream speech, waking testimony, felt violation, and established historical events without dismissing the experience or engineering a recovered memory. When an old harmful behavior recurs after extended stability, investigate what changed and what it temporarily provided instead of asking again whether the person wishes to stop. Test single-root-cause theories against existing low-symptom periods and reciprocal maintenance; evaluate a coach's useful support separately from product or detox claims. In a finished reply, cover each practice/topic once unless an additional mention changes an action or handles a separate urgent safety need. Consolidation must not delete necessary facts or actual safety boundaries.

**Authority and propagation:** owner instruction 2026-10-25 (private specifics excluded); public canonical summary `AMEND.CROSS.RESPONSE_FIDELITY_20261025`, six updated existing cross-guide nodes, `src/prompts/response-fidelity.mjs`, the served `RESPONSE-FIDELITY.md`, app shared prompt, therapy skill and source-map projection. `tests/response-fidelity.test.mjs` and graph/protocol checks demonstrate source/route delivery and fixed test expectations, not provider adherence, clinical validity or production deployment. No change to public-guide prose until queued downstream review.

## 2026-10-17 — A word check finds the same words, not the same point

Development-candidate lesson (not an installed-runtime claim): a read-only ChatGPT audit after #176 and #177 went live showed that the known-versus-new check, which ranks the record's entries by shared word forms, finds no close match when a journal rewords an established point: in synthetic text, "harsh self-criticism after mistakes" in the record and "tearing into myself" in a journal share no words. The flag was named `record_holds_close_match`, so false read as "the record doesn't hold this", the opposite of what a reviewer should conclude. The flag is now `close_word_match`; the tool's description, its answer and the private continuity skill say that no close match never makes a point new by itself; and the behavior test has a case where the check finds no close match for three reworded known points, graded on whether the reply calls them new. The same audit found a reconstructed plan listing no routes tried where a case's interventions came from older records; it now counts them and says an empty list doesn't mean nothing was tried.

Authority and evidence: issue #172 (the post-deploy audit, 17 Oct 2026, 21:57 UTC), `tasks/post-deploy-audit-20261017/INTEGRATION.md`; `tests/case-roadmap.test.mjs`, `tests/case-roadmap-mcp.test.mjs` and `tests/protocol-application-eval.test.mjs` (`journal-review-paraphrased-known`).

## 2026-10-17 — The person's own safety steps stay with reparenting, even next to a service

Development-candidate lesson (not an installed-runtime claim): with the served rules of #173, a behavior test on synthetic cases (Claude answering, Codex grading, 3 replies per case) still showed the split in 1 of 12 replies, and in 2 of 12 on the earlier rules. Each time the reply sorted its review by topic and put the person's own protective steps (a changed route, a colleague walking her to the car, a night at a friend's) under a "Safety and legal (a separate service)" heading or a "practical help" track. The rule invited it: it listed "concrete help from other people" and "safety services" as separate next to "arranging practical safety" as reparenting. The served rule now says that what stays separate is the service, or the other person's help, itself, never the person's own step, and that sorting by topic doesn't move the person's own steps under a services heading; `REPARENTING_FALSE_CHOICE` names that filing. Codex answering didn't make the split on either version.

Authority and evidence: the owner's answer 43a of 2026-10-17, `AMEND.IC.OWN_PROTECTIVE_STEPS_WITH_REPARENTING_20261017`, `tasks/services-boundary-20261017/INTEGRATION.md`; `tests/reparenting-framing.test.mjs` (`supervisor-own-steps-under-services`) and the protocol application test of #175 (owner's answers 42c and 43a).

## 2026-10-17 — Check what the record already holds before calling anything new

Development-candidate lesson (not an installed-runtime claim): after a journal import, a supervisor-facing review called a theme the case record had held for many sessions a new idea, did not say what the journal changed in the next step or the order of priorities, and set everyday protection and practical safety against reparenting again. More material made the old theme look new, and the rules for telling them apart lived only in the app's own prompts: the served rules did not carry them, and reached a host only if it loaded the protocol. Before presenting anything in a review across sessions, records or a journal as new, check it against the record and say whether it is established, a new example, a contradiction, a new lever or a change to what comes first; report what changes a decision, not how many more examples there are; read a journal's model-made labels as search aids; keep each priority's source; let safety and withdrawn consent override the order. Keeping oneself safe in daily life is part of the reparenting work when that frame is welcome; outside medical, crisis, safety and practical help stays its own thing and is not an exercise.

Authority and evidence: the owner's issue #172 of 2026-10-17, `AMEND.CROSS.KNOWN_VERSUS_NEW_SYNTHESIS_20261017`, `AMEND.IC.PROTECTIVE_ACTS_ARE_REPARENTING_20261017`, `tasks/case-plan-known-new-20261017/INTEGRATION.md`; `tests/longitudinal-synthesis.test.mjs`, `tests/reparenting-framing.test.mjs` and `tests/therapy-protocol-mcp.test.mjs` check delivery, not model adherence. Whether the reviewing chat had loaded the protocol is unknown; the connector now records which tools are called and when, without content.

## 2026-10-17 — Say what reparenting is before saying how one exercise is evidenced

Development-candidate lesson (not an installed-runtime claim): the served rules kept careful evidence for one exercise ("a good hypothetical caring response establishes accessible response content, not completed reparenting … invite the actual imaginal child/adult exercise"), and nothing served said what reparenting is, so a reader could take that sentence as the definition: reparenting as an imaginal exercise that can be completed, and feeling safe while expressing feelings, needs and boundaries as a separate goal it might support. A supervisor answer framed reparenting that way; the conversation isn't in the repository, so this is a contributing cause, not a proven sole one. Reparenting is the ongoing relationship in which the present-day adult cares for, validates, protects and guides the vulnerable younger self, through boundaries, repair and ordinary-life choices as well as any meditation, which is one method within it. Keep exercise evidence (taught, attempted, response reported, impact unknown) apart from the relationship's evidence (what the person does for the vulnerable self in daily life, and what it changed); infer neither progress from the right words nor absence from missing imagery, breakthrough or relief. Also check that the rule is loaded where the mistake happens: a trigger written for "therapy responses" can miss a supervisor reviewing a person's writing.

Authority and evidence: the owner's correction of 2026-10-16 (issue #168), `AMEND.IC.REPARENTING_NOT_AN_EXERCISE_20261016`, `tasks/reparenting-not-exercise-20261016/INTEGRATION.md`; `tests/reparenting-framing.test.mjs` checks delivery to every model role, the served MCP route and the unchanged child-contact gate. No live model was run, so these are not behavior results.

## 2026-10-06 — Ask the inner-speech question before the first inner dialogue, not at the start

Development-main lesson (not an installed-runtime claim): the served protocol asked the inner-speech screen "near the beginning of the first substantive therapy interaction", so on a first conversation replies asked it on almost any turn, including straight after something painful. Ask it once, before the first inner-dialogue or younger-self exercise, or earlier only when how the person thinks clearly matters for the next step. Skip it when the answer is already in the conversation or the supplied records. Never ask it in reply to something painful the person has just shared, or ahead of a safety or urgent issue; respond to what they said first. The question itself is unchanged.

Authority and evidence: owner decision 10: A of 2026-10-06 (owner questions page), approving the exact wording; `tasks/inner-speech-timing-20261006/` sample-reply rerun on the twelve invented first conversations: 2 of 24 replies asked it, both in the conversation that asked for an inner-child exercise and after acknowledging the feeling, against 16 of 24 with the old wording on 2026-10-04. Sample replies from one model on invented conversations are not live or clinical results.

## 2026-10-03 — Treat altered-state access as a transfer problem before reflexive repetition

Development-candidate lesson (not an installed-runtime claim): when an altered or nonordinary state makes an inner-child relationship, compassion, grief, protection, or another therapeutic insight experientially credible but sober access later fades, preserve the experience without either dismissing it or promoting it into external historical/metaphysical proof. Distinguish genuinely new exploration from repeating mainly to restore access to a known state, conviction, love, or relationship. For restore-access cases, prepare the relevant adult capacities beforehand and work explicitly on sober carryover afterward; do not assume sober work can replace altered-state methods for every person, and do not infer addiction, avoidance, or failed integration from repetition alone.

Authority and evidence: owner-authorized development direction of 2026-10-03, AMEND.CROSS.STATE_DEPENDENT_TRANSFER, ROUTE.ALTERED_STATE_TRANSFER, and graph regressions G047/G048. Deterministic tests establish routing behavior, not clinical efficacy or a claim that preparation reduces the number of altered-state sessions.

## 2026-09-26 — Review self-care attempts without holding a trial

Development-main closeout (not an installed-runtime claim): after an attempt at improved care, protection, or guidance, help the person notice without harsh judgment what felt right and what could be better next time; when an effort does not go as hoped, name what happened, repair what can be repaired, and make the next promise more credible. Review is voluntary and never grading, self-surveillance, or a verdict about worth, and it can honestly conclude that a commitment is too big right now. No review cadence is imposed.

Authority and evidence: owner decision D09 (2026-08-29, reaffirmed 2026-09-26), reconciled into `IC.ADULT_APPRENTICE`, `IC.CREDIBILITY_REPAIR`, and `IC.PROTECTOR_ACTION`; `tasks/nonpunitive-review-20260926/` and `tests/nonpunitive-review.test.mjs`. Tests are not clinical results.

## 2026-08-12T03:29:10.000Z — Preserve the user's distinctions before choosing an intervention

<!-- therapy-lesson {"lessonId":"active-formulation-distinctions","learnedAt":"2026-08-12T03:29:10.000Z","activation":"active-runtime"} -->

The formulation prompt now treats exact user language as evidence and keeps uncertain inner-role assignments as hypotheses. It distinguishes literal childhood, adolescence, younger adulthood, a present child-state, and chronological adulthood; witness capacity is tracked separately from a stable Nurturer/Protector/Guide capacity. This reduces the risk of deciding who a voice “is” before the user has supplied enough evidence.

Evidence: `src/prompts/case-extract.mjs`, `src/prompts/case-audit.mjs`, and A001 acceptance rules.

## 2026-08-12T03:29:10.000Z — Treat credibility as an evidence problem, not merely an arousal problem

<!-- therapy-lesson {"lessonId":"active-credibility-evidence","learnedAt":"2026-08-12T03:29:10.000Z","activation":"active-runtime"} -->

When a younger position points to how adult life actually turned out, the prompt must preserve an adverse track record rather than saying there is no track record yet. Regulation may help the person remain with the conflict, but it cannot be presented as though relaxation alone repairs credibility. Sarcasm such as “what are you going to do for me?” is handled as a practical request for observable evidence: one bounded act, no demand for immediate trust, then repeated follow-through.

Evidence: `src/prompts/realize.mjs`, `corpus/difficult-cases/A001-inner-child-credibility/case.json`, and graph node `IC.CREDIBILITY_REPAIR`.

## 2026-08-12T03:29:10.000Z — Spend deeper review only where ambiguity or safety requires it

<!-- therapy-lesson {"lessonId":"active-tiered-review","learnedAt":"2026-08-12T03:29:10.000Z","activation":"active-runtime"} -->

Low-ambiguity prompts can follow the graph quickly. Protective conflict or moderate ambiguity receives independent review. When age, responsibility, speaker, and credibility ambiguity is fully represented in the structured graph variables, reviewed mode can resolve it without unnecessary Opus depth; an unresolved or unstructured version receives deeper review. A safety-sensitive state cannot be forced into fast mode. A narrow follow-up to an already reviewed formulation can update the prior snapshot instead of rebuilding the person from scratch.

Evidence: `src/orchestrator/run-tiered-pipeline.mjs` and `tests/therapy-routing.test.mjs`.

## 2026-08-12T03:29:10.000Z — Keep the response focused and do not manufacture generic caution

<!-- therapy-lesson {"lessonId":"active-focused-realization","learnedAt":"2026-08-12T03:29:10.000Z","activation":"active-runtime"} -->

The final prompt should lead with the live knot, answer the user's own actionable question, give one main next move, and preserve the graph-owned discriminating question exactly. Generic crisis, grounding, dissociation, or recovered-memory boilerplate is omitted when the deterministic case variables do not trigger it. Every claimed intervention must have an exact supporting quote in the rendered answer.

Evidence: `src/prompts/realize.mjs` response-realization contract.

